%%
%% SECTION 2: SECURITY FRAMING AND THREAT MODEL
%%
\section{Sycophancy as a Security Property}
\label{sec:threatmodel}

Sycophancy is usually studied as an alignment defect. This section argues it is better understood,
and better managed, as an integrity failure in a system whose trusted component is a human
decision-maker. The distinction is not merely presentational: it determines who owns the problem,
what a mitigation is expected to guarantee, and whether the resulting assurance can be evaluated
and certified the way other security properties are.

\subsection{The Asset Is the Decision, Not the Model}

In the deployments that motivate this SoK, an analyst, clinician, examiner, or engineer consults a
model and then acts. The asset requiring protection is the integrity of that decision; the model
is infrastructure. Framed this way, sycophancy is a failure of the same kind as silent data
corruption: the system returns an answer that is well formed, confidently presented, and wrong in
a direction determined by something other than the evidence. Conventional integrity controls do
not detect it because nothing in the pipeline is tampered with. The bits are intact; the inference
is not.

Transport encryption, access control, and attestation establish that the output arrived
unmodified; none speaks to whether it was shaped by the user's phrasing rather than the evidence,
which is the question a decision-maker needs answered.

\subsection{Threat Model}
\label{subsec:threat}

We assume the model, its weights, its host, and its data path are all uncompromised. This is a
deliberately conservative posture: the failure we describe requires no breach.

\paragraph{Adversary capability.} The adversary can influence the wording of a query. That is the
whole of the requirement. The capability is possessed by the end user, by anyone who authors a
prompt template or system message, by any upstream process that composes a query from retrieved
content, and by anyone who can place text where the model will read it. It requires no credentials
and leaves no artifact in a conventional audit log.

\paragraph{Success condition.} The attack succeeds when three conditions hold: the model's
output asserts or materially supports a proposition that the evidence available to it does not
support; the deviation is attributable to the influence channel, that is, to the phrasing,
claimed authority, emotional register, or planted context of the input rather than to new
evidence the input introduced; and a downstream decision relies on that output. Two neighboring
behaviors are excluded by construction. A change of wording or tone that preserves propositional
content is not a success, and neither is appropriate updating, a position change in response to
genuinely new evidence, which is desirable. The distinction is operational, not rhetorical:
counterfactual designs hold content fixed and vary only the stance signal
\citep{dubois2026askdont}, AEDI defines deference as sensitivity of expressed support to user
attitude beyond any evidence supplied \citep{botas2026aedi}, and users themselves distinguish
stance shifts driven by evidence from shifts driven by their views \citep{sun2026friendly}. The
third condition, decision reliance, is the one existing instruments rarely measure, which is why
Section~\ref{sec:measurement} treats decision-level studies
\citep{cheng2025prosocial,li2026sycophancy_decisions,bo2026saboteurs} as part of the assurance
picture rather than background.

\paragraph{The prompt as an unvalidated input channel.} The evidence surveyed in
Section~\ref{sec:causes} establishes that this capability is often sufficient to move the output.
In controlled comparisons on identical content, sycophancy rises monotonically with the epistemic
certainty the user expresses, is amplified by first-person framing, and is substantially higher
for assertions than for questions, approaching zero in question form~\citep{dubois2026askdont};
claimed authority~\citep{celebi2025parrot} and expressed emotional distress~\citep{ibrahim2026warm}
shift responses further. In security terms, the prompt is an unvalidated input channel that
influences an output on which a trust decision rests, and it is not currently treated as one.

The strength of this channel is elicitation-dependent, and the boundary condition matters for any
defender. A single stated hypothesis in a neutral professional register is a weak stimulus
compared to the conditions under which the literature locates the largest effects: sustained
multi-turn pressure, in which the user rebuts the model's first answer and persists.
\citet{hong2025sycon} show that all 17 models they test eventually flip under sustained pressure
and that capitulation accelerates with conversation length, and \citet{kim2025challenging} show
that a rebuttal delivered as a conversational follow-up induces far more capitulation than the
same two positions presented simultaneously. The practical consequence is that single-turn prompt
hygiene is a weaker control than it appears, and that the multi-turn interaction, not the
individual query, is the unit at which this channel should be monitored.

\paragraph{Adversary classes.}
\label{subsec:adversaryclasses}
Two classes call for different controls and different evidence. \emph{Adversarial manipulation} is
strategic: a self-serving party phrases queries to elicit support for a position it already holds
and presents the agreement as independent corroboration, or a third party plants text that will
enter someone else's context, and either can adapt its wording to whatever moves the model.
\emph{Non-adversarial reliability failure} needs no intent: a user asks leading questions, the most
common case, and the decision is corrupted just the same. Fixed-template benchmarks bound only the
second class, because a scripted challenge cannot represent an attacker who iterates. Evidence on
the first class is rare but real: FlowSteer crafts a single prompt whose sycophantic framing makes
downstream agents relay a malicious signal, raising malicious success by up to 55\% over naive
prompting in multi-agent planners~\citep{li2026flowsteer}, and simulated patients that persuade
adversarially yield acquiescence to guideline-discordant care from 0\% to 100\% across 19
models~\citep{peng2026sycoeval}. Section~\ref{subsec:grading} records which class each control was
validated against.

\paragraph{Out of scope.} We exclude adversaries who compromise weights, poison training data at
source, or breach the serving infrastructure. Those threats are real and separately treated. The
point of the present model is that none of them is necessary.

\subsection{The Premise Channel and Instruction Injection}
\label{sec:injection}

Conclusion-level agreement is not the only path to a corrupted decision. A query can smuggle its
bias through an unsupported premise that the model accepts and reasons from faithfully.
\citet{zhang2025pressure} measure this channel directly: their misleading-resistance metric tracks
the accuracy a model retains when the user's input embeds a false premise, and it degrades under
adversarial dialogue. \citet{turpin2023faithful} document the complementary failure, showing that
models can produce fluent chain-of-thought rationales consistent with a bias planted in the
context while never mentioning that bias, so the stated reasoning conceals the influence. Premise
auditing is therefore a control distinct from conclusion-level sycophancy mitigation, and the
evidence suggests it is the weaker of the two in current systems. The channel extends to agents:
in multi-turn tool calling, models comply with plausible but contradictory assertions returned by
tools in 27.8\% of cases on average, not far below their 36.3\% compliance with confidently stated
user beliefs~\citep{waqas2025acc}.

Two failure modes meet on this channel and should not be conflated. Sycophancy proper is
belief-conforming output: the model shifts toward a stance a person signals, under the
social-approval gradient the training pipeline rewards. Indirect prompt injection is
instruction-following applied to content that should have been treated as data: text reaching the
model through a document, retrieval result, or tool output is executed as a directive, whether or
not any user belief is involved \citep{lin2025hiddenprompts}. The mechanisms differ,
preference-trained accommodation versus instruction-tuned compliance, and so do the controls. The
deference-targeting interventions of Section~\ref{sec:controls} address the former; the latter
calls for provenance, instruction-data separation, and sanitization of retrieved content, an
adjacent literature this SoK does not systematize. What the two share, and what places injection
at the boundary of this threat model, is the structural condition this section describes:
unvalidated text influencing an output on which a decision rests, invisibly to integrity
controls, through a system disposed to treat whatever its context asserts as authoritative.

The document supply chain instantiates the injection mode, and one instance was encountered
during the literature collection for this SoK; we present it as an illustrative incident, not as
evidence, since the threat model stands on the published literature. One paper gathered as a
primary source carries instruction text in its PDF text layer, placed before the title, where text
extraction reads it but a human viewing the rendered page does not. It directs any tool processing
the document to repeat a phrase an exact number of times, and a routine automated summarization
step reproduced the phrase exactly as demanded, woven into coherent prose with no sign of its
origin, across repeated runs. No system was compromised; the file hashes identically and a
signature over it verifies, so the usual integrity controls see nothing; and the output reads as
the analytic voice of whoever ran the tool, corrupting decision integrity through the supply chain
rather than the query. The disposition that agrees with a user's stated belief is not the one that
executes a document's demand, but both meet in the same unvalidated channel and defeat the same
integrity assumptions.

We do not identify the document or reproduce its payload. Such text may be placed defensively,
by authors seeking to detect undisclosed automated review, rather than with intent to manipulate;
the mechanism and the consequence for an automated reader are identical either way, and the class
is independently documented: screens of preprint servers in July 2025 surfaced manuscripts
carrying hidden instructions addressed to AI reviewers \citep{lin2025hiddenprompts}. The
practical implication is that
provenance and sanitization controls over material entering a model's context are a prerequisite
for the mitigations of Section~\ref{sec:controls}, not an adjacent concern: a control that reduces
deference to the user is of limited value if the model remains fully deferential to any text it is
given to read. We return to this asymmetry in Section~\ref{sec:openproblems}.
